"639 CHAPTER 11. PLURALITY OF OBLIGORS AND OF OBLIGEES Section 1. Plurality of Obligors 11.1.1 Definitions 11.1.2 Presumption of joint and several obligations 11.1.3 Obligee’s rights against joint and several obligors 11.1.4 Availability of defences and rights of set-off 11.1.5 Effect of performance or set-off 11.1.6 Effect of release or settlement 11.1.7 Effect of expiration or suspension of limitation period 11.1.8 Effect of judgment 11.1.9 Apportionment among joint and several obligors 11.1.10 Extent of contributory claim 11.1.11 Rights of the obligee 11.1.12 Defences in contributory claims 11.1.13 Inability to recover Section 2. Plurality of Obligees 11.2.1 Definitions 11.2.2 Effects of joint and several claims 11.2.3 Availability of defences against joint and several obligees 11.2.4 Allocation between joint and several obligees PREAMBLE (Purpose of the Principles) These Principles set forth general rules for international commercial contracts. They shall be applied when the parties have agreed that their contract be governed by them.(*) They may be applied when the parties have agreed that their contract be governed by general principles of law, the lex mercatoria or the like. They may be applied when the parties have not chosen any law to govern their contract. They may be used to interpret or supplement international uniform law instruments. They may be used to interpret or supplement domestic law. They may serve as a model for national and international legislators. (*) Parties wishing to provide that their agreement be governed by the Principles might use one of the Model Clauses for the use of the UNIDROIT Principles of Inter- national Commercial Contracts (see http:// www.unidroit.org/instruments/commer- cial-contracts/upicc-model-clauses). INTERNATIONAL COMMERCIAL CONTRACTS"